# ============================================================================
# CLOUD BROKER ACL
#
# The topic scheme is site-first (sites/<site>/...) precisely so that this file
# can express the tenant boundary as a prefix. A site's credential is confined
# to its own subtree: a compromised site-1 gateway cannot publish telemetry as
# site 2, nor read site 2's commands.
# ============================================================================

# --- the cloud Spring Boot service ------------------------------------------
# Reads every site; writes only command requests.
user cloud-app
topic read  sites/+/telemetry/+
topic read  sites/+/health/+
topic read  sites/+/command/+/res
topic write sites/+/command/+/req
topic read  sites/+/bridge/state
topic read  $SYS/#

# --- site 1 gateway bridge --------------------------------------------------
# Note this cannot collapse to `sites/site1/#`: the bridge must WRITE telemetry
# and health but only READ commands, and granting write on the whole subtree
# would let a site inject commands addressed to its own devices -- bypassing the
# cloud entirely, and with it every authorisation check that lives there.
user bridge-site1
topic write sites/site1/telemetry/+
topic write sites/site1/health/+
topic write sites/site1/command/+/res
topic read  sites/site1/command/+/req
topic write sites/site1/bridge/state

# --- site 2 gateway bridge --------------------------------------------------
user bridge-site2
topic write sites/site2/telemetry/+
topic write sites/site2/health/+
topic write sites/site2/command/+/res
topic read  sites/site2/command/+/req
topic write sites/site2/bridge/state

# --- read-only inspection account (MQTT Explorer UI) ------------------------
# Sees everything, changes nothing. With a site-first scheme this is one line.
# Kept separate from cloud-app so the debugging tool can never publish a command
# by accident, and so its access can be revoked without touching the service.
user observer
topic read sites/#
topic read $SYS/#
